</ol>

<h2>Weather Factors That Affect Shingles in Temple and Bell County</h2>
<p>Shingles age fastest under the conditions Central Texas delivers in abundance. Long stretches of triple-digit summer heat and strong UV dry out the asphalt and loosen granules. Spring storms, roughly March through June, bring hail that can bruise or crack shingles and winds that can lift tabs and break the adhesive seal strips. A secondary storm season in the fall adds more wear. Occasional hard freezes, like the February 2021 winter storm, can expose weak spots where water has worked under shingles.</p>
<p>Correct nailing and properly sealed shingles make a meaningful difference in wind resistance, which is why installation quality matters as much as the product. If your roof has already taken a hit from a storm, our <a href="/services/hail-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">hail damage roofing</a> page covers what happens next, and our <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">guide to spotting hail damage</a> shows what to look for from the ground.</p>

<h2>When to Have Your Shingle Roof Inspected</h2>
<ul>
<li>After any hail or high-wind event in your area</li>
<li>When the roof reaches roughly 15 years old, or earlier for 3-tab shingles</li>
<li>Before listing or buying a home</li>
<li>If you notice granules collecting in gutters or new ceiling stains</li>
</ul>
